## Why the recovery loop cannot settle silent input

[screen.rs:564](/work/reference-code/lanthorn/crates/zvm-cli/src/screen.rs:564) suppresses unchanged upper-window blocks. Without an active numeric jump, [menu.rs:450](/work/reference-code/lanthorn/crates/cli-host/src/menu.rs:450) returns no menu emission. Returning from lower-window topic text to the same upper-window menu can therefore be silent (inference; not a replay of these stories).

[play_broker.py:75](/work/experiments/lanthorn-recall-training/scripts/play_broker.py:75) checks `waiting_for_stdin()` only when `buf` is nonempty. With zero output it does not even probe readiness and inevitably returns timeout at line 79. `send` latches unresolved_input at lines 101–102. `await_output` repeats the same drain at lines 109–118, clearing the latch only after a non-timeout result. More passive waits cannot settle a permanently silent input read. Retain the no-replay/no-restart guards; this finding does not justify clearing them from silence alone.

## Harness-only options and limits

1. Immediate operational containment: avoid optional menus on future paths where possible. Treat this exact empty-response menu signature as a parked transport issue instead of spending repeated passive 180-second waits on it. Do not invent an Enter, q, backspace, repeated SPACE or save as a flush key. Required menu content still needs an explicit resolution.
2. A source-supported future harness mechanism is an exact host-only `/menu` query. [main.rs:1064](/work/reference-code/lanthorn/crates/zvm-cli/src/main.rs:1064) intercepts it in the plain character reader; [main.rs:2122](/work/reference-code/lanthorn/crates/zvm-cli/src/main.rs:2122) also intercepts it at ordinary line input. Both answer without supplying a game command. [main.rs:568](/work/reference-code/lanthorn/crates/zvm-cli/src/main.rs:568) prints and flushes the answer. It may report a menu or `[no menu is open]`; it does not prove the prior game action succeeded.
3. That mechanism is **not currently available through the broker**: slash validation rejects it at line 93 and the unresolved-input guard prevents another send at lines 95–96. Any later harness implementation should be a narrowly typed, separately logged host query, not general slash allowance or input replay. Restrict it to the verified central plain-menu build and a proven eligible input stop; preserve session ownership and the original unresolved event. File-name questions and non-plain character readers are not covered by these host-command interceptions. If eligibility cannot be proven, leave the session parked. Do not enqueue a speculative query behind uncertain input.
4. Another future harness improvement is explicit reporting of a silent, proven stdin wait without calling it game success. Do not simply remove the `buf` guard and automatically clear uncertainty based on one syscall snapshot: it does not identify NeedChar versus NeedLine/filename input or prove that the just-written request has been consumed. A safe implementation needs consumption/read-boundary evidence, plus isolated validation before deployment.

No reliable universal replacement key or currently deployable recovery is established by this bounded read-only audit. Host-query behavior is source-supported, not runtime-tested here. No current-live fix is claimed.
